Apple Store button was visually oversized relative to Google Play, and on mobile the store buttons were overflowing into the next section due to an unoverridden inline `marginBottom: '-40px'` on the actions container.

## Changes

- **Apple Store button**: reduced desktop height `56px → 50px`
- **Mobile overlap fix**: override the inline `-40px` bottom margin via CSS `!important` in both mobile breakpoints (`≤768px: 32px`, `≤480px: 24px`)

```css
/* ≤768px */
.actions {
  margin-bottom: 32px !important;
}

/* ≤480px */
.actions {
  margin-bottom: 24px !important;
}
```

Base `home.nix` set `enableZshIntegration = true` (default priority) for starship/zoxide/fzf, conflicting with user-specific overrides (e.g. `laercio-home.nix` disabling starship's Zsh integration in favour of powerlevel10k) at the same priority level.

## Changes

- **`home.nix`**: Wrap `enableZshIntegration`, `enableFishIntegration`, and `enableBashIntegration` with `lib.mkDefault` for `starship`, `zoxide`, and `fzf`

```nix
starship = {
  enable = true;
  enableZshIntegration = lib.mkDefault true;  # was: true
  enableFishIntegration = lib.mkDefault true;
  enableBashIntegration = lib.mkDefault true;
  # ...
};
```

`lib.mkDefault` lowers the definition priority so any plain assignment in a user module silently wins, without requiring `lib.mkForce` on the override side. This is the standard pattern for shared base modules in NixOS/home-manager.

